"Air takes a long trip to get to your blood. It enters through your nose, where tiny hairs and sticky mucus catch dust, and where the air is warmed and made moist. It passes down your throat into the trachea, or windpipe, a tube held open by rings of cartilage that you can feel at the front of your neck. The trachea splits into two bronchi, one for each lung, and they split again and again, more than 20 times, into thousands of tiny tubes called bronchioles. Doctors call this the bronchial tree, because it looks like a tree turned upside down.": "e16d68154f3c",
"At the very end of the smallest branches are the alveoli, tiny air sacs that grow in bunches like grapes. You have hundreds of millions of them. Spread flat, their surface would cover about the floor of a classroom. Each air sac is wrapped in a net of capillaries, and the walls between the air and the blood are thinner than a soap bubble. Oxygen slips across into the blood, and carbon dioxide slips out into the air sac. Then the heart, which you met in Lesson 3, carries the fresh, oxygen-rich blood to the rest of your body.": "35254d3377b5",
"Breathe in slowly through your nose.": "f49e8360e5c8",
"Each tiny air sac is wrapped in blood vessels.": "fa1213e87718",
"Exhale slowly and let your shoulders relax.": "2673a8bb7bc3",
"Here is the surprise: your lungs cannot pull in air by themselves. They have no muscles of their own. The work is done by the diaphragm, a dome-shaped sheet of muscle under your lungs. When you breathe in, the diaphragm tightens and flattens, moving down, while small muscles between your ribs lift the ribs up and out. Your chest gets bigger, the air pressure inside your lungs drops, and air rushes in to fill the space. When you breathe out quietly, the diaphragm relaxes and domes back up, and your stretchy lungs spring back, like a balloon letting go of its air. The hand on your belly moved because the diaphragm pushed down on everything below it.": "0516fc474152",
"If the lungs have no muscles, what makes air come in when you breathe in?": "3257df1b480f",
"Inhale deeply before you dive into the pool.": "0a2791d1e791",
"Most adults at rest breathe about 12 to 20 times a minute, and children breathe a little faster. Your lungs keep growing until you are about 20 years old, so the choices you make now matter. Smoke, whether from cigarettes, burning incense in a closed room, or polluted air, damages the delicate air sacs, while regular exercise strengthens the muscles that help you breathe. Now count your breaths for 30 seconds, and let the lungs in the model breathe with you.": "e949ebc33538",
"Plants take in carbon dioxide and give off oxygen.": "adea83e7b4d9",
"Put one hand on your belly and take a slow, deep breath. You probably felt your belly push out. You breathe more than 20,000 times a day, usually without thinking about it at all. A person can live for weeks without food and for days without water, but only a few minutes without air. Every one of your cells needs a steady supply of oxygen, and your lungs are where your body gets it.": "ba6053341cbf",
"Put one hand on your belly and take a slow, deep breath. You probably felt your belly push out. You breathe more than 20,000 times a day, usually without thinking about it at all. A person can live for weeks without food and for days without water, but only a few minutes without air. Every one of your cells needs a steady supply of oxygen, and your lungs are where your body gets it. Here is the surprise: your lungs cannot pull in air by themselves. They have no muscles of their own. The work is done by the diaphragm, a dome-shaped sheet of muscle under your lungs. When you breathe in, the diaphragm tightens and flattens, moving down, while small muscles between your ribs lift the ribs up and out. Your chest gets bigger, the air pressure inside your lungs drops, and air rushes in to fill the space. When you breathe out quietly, the diaphragm relaxes and domes back up, and your stretchy lungs spring back, like a balloon letting go of its air. The hand on your belly moved because the diaphragm pushed down on everything below it. Air takes a long trip to get to your blood. It enters through your nose, where tiny hairs and sticky mucus catch dust, and where the air is warmed and made moist. It passes down your throat into the trachea, or windpipe, a tube held open by rings of cartilage that you can feel at the front of your neck. The trachea splits into two bronchi, one for each lung, and they split again and again, more than 20 times, into thousands of tiny tubes called bronchioles. Doctors call this the bronchial tree, because it looks like a tree turned upside down. At the very end of the smallest branches are the alveoli, tiny air sacs that grow in bunches like grapes. You have hundreds of millions of them. Spread flat, their surface would cover about the floor of a classroom. Each air sac is wrapped in a net of capillaries, and the walls between the air and the blood are thinner than a soap bubble. Oxygen slips across into the blood, and carbon dioxide slips out into the air sac. Then the heart, which you met in Lesson 3, carries the fresh, oxygen-rich blood to the rest of your body. The air you breathe in is about 21 percent oxygen. Surprisingly, the air you breathe out still has about 16 percent oxygen, but it carries about 100 times more carbon dioxide than it did coming in. Your cells use oxygen to release the energy stored in food, and carbon dioxide is what is left over. In fact, what makes you feel that you need to breathe is mostly a rise in carbon dioxide in your blood, which your brain keeps track of all the time. That is why you breathe faster and deeper when you run. Most adults at rest breathe about 12 to 20 times a minute, and children breathe a little faster. Your lungs keep growing until you are about 20 years old, so the choices you make now matter. Smoke, whether from cigarettes, burning incense in a closed room, or polluted air, damages the delicate air sacs, while regular exercise strengthens the muscles that help you breathe. Now count your breaths for 30 seconds, and let the lungs in the model breathe with you.": "716572893099",
